content:
The evidence does not provide Somalia-specific data or a dated assessment of current access to livestock processing or animal-health services. Therefore, Somalia’s present status and recent trend cannot be established from these guidelines alone. The guidelines indicate that small-scale livestock producers commonly face restricted access to markets and related services, limited capacities, poor-quality inputs such as veterinary drugs, inadequate expertise, high transaction costs, and low sanitary standards [p. 19]. They identify livestock processing as including aggregation, slaughter, transformation, storage, refrigeration, and distribution, with facilities such as abattoirs, milk tanks, dairy plants, and tanneries potentially crucial to the value chain [pp. 27–28]. Access is also shaped by infrastructure—including roads, transport, cold chains, markets, ports, electricity, and ICT—which affects processing and distribution [pp. 65–66]. For animal health, the guidelines recommend assessing the availability and reliability of veterinary drugs, vaccines, acaricides, insecticides, and other treatments, as well as identifying whether services are provided by public, private, or community-based providers [p. 70]. They also stress that veterinary care is needed to maintain production, prevent losses, and reduce zoonotic disease transmission [p. 27]. Recent trends should be measured using production and market volumes, prices, employment, output, and growth rates over time; the value-chain map can depict both the current situation and changes at each link [p. 76].

